1
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:03,567
Every day... 800 million times.

2
00:00:03,800 --> 00:00:08,000
Someone buys tea... someone fills petrol... someone sends rent.

3
00:00:08,200 --> 00:00:12,567
One QR code. One scan. And in two seconds, the money arrives.

4
00:00:12,767 --> 00:00:15,900
No fee. No charge. Completely free.

5
00:00:16,267 --> 00:00:21,067
But the world's largest real-time payment system doesn't run for free.

6
00:00:21,500 --> 00:00:25,767
Someone is paying its bill, every single day.

7
00:00:26,667 --> 00:00:28,500
The question is: who?

8
00:00:39,933 --> 00:00:41,767
Every magic trick has three parts.

9
00:00:42,100 --> 00:00:47,133
Part one: the pledge. The magician shows you something perfectly ordinary.

10
00:00:48,933 --> 00:00:51,833
In our story, that ordinary thing is... this.

11
00:00:54,467 --> 00:01:01,200
August 2016. NPCI's new system, the Unified Payments Interface, opens to the public with 21 banks.

12
00:01:01,500 --> 00:01:04,933
In that first full month, there aren't even a million transactions.

13
00:01:06,933 --> 00:01:13,733
Then comes 1 January 2020. The government makes MDR zero on UPI and RuPay debit cards.

14
00:01:14,067 --> 00:01:23,000
MDR, the Merchant Discount Rate: the small fee a shop pays to accept a digital payment. On UPI it used to be up to 0.30%.

15
00:01:24,467 --> 00:01:27,467
And then... UPI explodes.

16
00:01:28,433 --> 00:01:37,267
September 2026: 24 billion transactions in a month. On average, more than 800 million a day.

17
00:01:37,600 --> 00:01:41,500
According to ACI Worldwide, almost half of the world's real-time payments happen in India.

18
00:01:43,467 --> 00:01:47,800
The pledge was kept. UPI was free, fast... everywhere.

19
00:01:48,133 --> 00:01:52,467
But in magic, what you see is never the whole truth.

20
00:01:57,933 --> 00:02:02,200
Part two: the turn. The magician makes the ordinary thing... disappear.

21
00:02:02,533 --> 00:02:05,733
In UPI, what disappeared was the bill.

22
00:02:07,933 --> 00:02:15,933
Behind your one scan runs an entire machine: your app, your bank, NPCI's switch, and the shop's bank.

23
00:02:16,267 --> 00:02:20,533
Servers, security, fraud checks, customer support, around the clock.

24
00:02:20,867 --> 00:02:25,133
None of it is free. So when the fee went to zero, where did the cost go?

25
00:02:26,933 --> 00:02:30,533
Answer one: the government. In other words, taxpayers' money.

26
00:02:30,867 --> 00:02:43,300
Per PIB data, the government paid banks incentives: ₹1,389 crore in 2021-22, rising to ₹3,631 crore by 2023-24.

27
00:02:44,933 --> 00:02:55,533
But the industry kept saying it isn't enough: per the Payments Council of India, running UPI costs about ₹10,000 crore a year, against only ₹1,500 crore of government support.

28
00:02:56,933 --> 00:03:00,067
And all this time, the government's answer was the same.

29
00:03:00,667 --> 00:03:06,467
August 2022, Finance Ministry: UPI is a digital public good; there is no plan to levy any charges.

30
00:03:06,967 --> 00:03:12,100
June 2025, Finance Ministry: reports of MDR on UPI are completely false, baseless and misleading.

31
00:03:12,800 --> 00:03:19,733
Just six weeks later, RBI Governor Sanjay Malhotra said: costs will have to be paid; someone will have to bear the cost.

32
00:03:21,933 --> 00:03:27,200
And in the 2026-27 budget, this support was cut by about 10% from the previous year.

33
00:03:27,533 --> 00:03:31,367
The bill hadn't vanished. It just wasn't visible to anyone.

34
00:03:36,933 --> 00:03:41,733
August 2026. Parliament amends the Payment and Settlement Systems Act, and on 17 August the President gives assent.

35
00:03:42,933 --> 00:03:47,900
14 September: the Finance Ministry's notification. The next day, the full rulebook.

36
00:03:48,933 --> 00:03:49,867
The rule is simple.

37
00:03:50,067 --> 00:03:57,067
Merchant payments above ₹2,000 carry a 0.4% MDR.

38
00:03:57,400 --> 00:04:00,833
Even on payments of ₹75,000 or more, at most ₹300.

39
00:04:01,167 --> 00:04:06,333
Railways, telecom, insurance and fuel: a flat ₹5.

40
00:04:06,667 --> 00:04:15,667
Person-to-person transfers stay free. Small merchants receiving up to ₹1 lakh a month via UPI pay nothing.

41
00:04:15,967 --> 00:04:19,500
Most importantly, the fee cannot be taken from the customer. The shop pays it.

42
00:04:21,467 --> 00:04:26,267
By NPCI's estimate, this could raise up to ₹20,600 crore a year.

43
00:04:27,933 --> 00:04:30,767
And then the countdown began.

44
00:04:31,367 --> 00:04:38,700
In a LocalCircles survey of over 32,000 merchants, only 17% said they would absorb the fee themselves.

45
00:04:39,033 --> 00:04:46,967
And of over 67,000 users, only 14% said they would stick with UPI if the fee were passed on to them.

46
00:04:48,933 --> 00:04:57,400
Madhya Pradesh's petrol pump dealers announced: from 16 October, no UPI above ₹2,000. Punjab and Haryana said the same.

47
00:04:58,467 --> 00:05:07,067
The case reached the Supreme Court. On 28 September it refused a stay and asked the Centre, RBI and NPCI to reply within four weeks.

48
00:05:07,400 --> 00:05:12,433
And the government told the court: it is neither a tax nor a fee.

49
00:05:14,467 --> 00:05:19,867
Then, just two days before the deadline, on 13 October, NPCI announced:

50
00:05:20,367 --> 00:05:24,633
MDR: after Diwali. The new date? Not decided yet.

51
00:05:30,467 --> 00:05:36,967
And now, part three: the prestige. The moment the vanished thing comes back.

52
00:05:37,733 --> 00:05:42,300
The bill that vanished in 2020 never really vanished.

53
00:05:43,100 --> 00:05:50,033
For years, part of it was paid with taxpayers' money, which means, in some way, your money. The rest was carried by banks and payment companies.

54
00:05:50,367 --> 00:05:54,200
Now the bill is back, on the shopkeeper's counter.

55
00:05:56,467 --> 00:06:04,167
The law says the charge can't be put on you. But economics says every business cost eventually shows up somewhere in prices.

56
00:06:04,467 --> 00:06:14,933
So who pays for free UPI? As a taxpayer, you. As a customer, perhaps you. And if the system weakens, the loss is yours too.

57
00:06:16,933 --> 00:06:24,333
The real question isn't whether UPI should stay free. It's who should pay for the world's largest digital payment system, how much, and how.

58
00:06:24,667 --> 00:06:31,600
On one side, the system needs money to run. On the other, millions of small shopkeepers, and the risk of a return to cash.

59
00:06:33,467 --> 00:06:39,867
For now, we wait for the new date. And the case is still pending in the Supreme Court.

60
00:06:40,167 --> 00:06:42,300
The coin... is still spinning.

61
00:06:47,467 --> 00:06:54,267
What do you think: is this fee on payments above ₹2,000 right or wrong? Tell us in the comments. All sources are in the description.
